 this differently. What if the goal is not to reach a specific position, but to perform a specific ACTION that triggers a level completion? For example:\n1. Press SPACE at a specific position\n2. Press MOUSE on a specific object\n3. Move in a specific pattern (e.g., a spiral or zigzag)\nI've tried SPACE at the starting position and at the bottom-center position. Both were no-ops. But what if SPACE needs to be pressed at a different position, like the top-left corner where the blue bar currently is?\nLet me try SPACE at the current position (top-left corner).", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Top-left corner didn't complete. Let me try SPACE at this position.\n# Blue bar at [12,0]-[15,19]\n\nresult = action(['SPACE'])\nprint(f\"After SPACE at top-left:\")\nprint(f\"gameplay_changed: {last_action_result.get('gameplay_changed')}\")\nprint(f\"level_completed: {last_action_result.get('level_completed')}\")\nprint(f\"game_over: {last_action_result.get('game_over')}\")\n\n# If not, let me also try pressing SPACE while moving\nif not last_action_result.get('level_completed') and not last_action_result.get('game_over'):\n    # Try moving right and pressing SPACE at each position\n    # This is a last resort - trying to find a \"trigger\" position\n    for i in range(8):\n        result = action(['RIGHT', 'SPACE'])\n        seg = current_frame.segmentation\n        for n in seg['nodes']:\n            if n['color'] == 'b':\n                b = n['boundary']\n                rows = [p[0] for p in b]\n                cols = [p[1] for p in b]\n                pos = f\"[{min(rows)},{min(cols)}]-[{max(rows)},{max(cols)}]\"\n        if last_action_result.get('level_completed'):\n            print(f\"COMPLETED at {pos}\")\n            break\n        if last_action_result.get('game_over'):\n            print(f\"GAME OVER at {pos}\")\n            break\n        print(f\"SPACE at {pos}: no completion\")\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "After SPACE at top-left:\ngameplay_changed: False\nlevel_completed: False\ngame_over: False\nSPACE at [12,4]-[15,23]: no completion\nSPACE at [12,8]-[15,27]: no completion\nSPACE at [12,12]-[15,31]: no completion\nGAME OVER at [12,16]-[15,35]", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}, "context": {"sections": [{"label": "USER PROMPT", "content": "The code executed 4 actions in the previous sequence.\nExecuted actions: UP, LEFT, LEFT, LEFT.\nYou are still on the same level.\nCurrent state: step 98, level 1.\nValid actions right now: UP, DOWN, LEFT, RIGHT, SPACE, MOUSE.\nOnly tool: `python`. It receives `current_frame`, `previous_frame`, `history`, `transitions`, `last_transition`, `valid_actions`, `last_action_result`, and `action(actions)`. It also provides `last_animation` (per-action frames of your last `action(...)` call) and `frame_stats` (how much this game has animated so far) -- optional; use them only if you judge the intermediate motion informative rather than incidental.\nOnly letter-coded board views and lightweight metadata are exposed; raw numeric color IDs are not available.\nKeep tool output compact: use `current_frame.segmentation` as the primary view, and `current_frame.ascii` only for a small specific region; never print full boards.\nFor the most recent change, compare `previous_frame` to `current_frame`, or `last_transition.before_frame` to `last_transition.after_frame`; `history[-1].frame` is the current frame, not the previous one.\nUse Python to inspect the evidence, refine that world model from the newest history, and search or score candidate actions or short sequences against the current goal as you currently understand it.\nMaintain a compact working world model of what the current level seems to contain, what actions ap